{
  "name": "What to Do When You Keep Tripping at Home",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "It's frustrating and sometimes scary when you find yourself tripping or losing your balance regularly in your own home. You might wonder which areas are most hazardous or if simple fixes are actually enough to keep you safe. Often, the biggest culprits are right in plain sight, like loose rugs, cords across walkways, or even unstable furniture. Poor lighting also plays a huge role, making it hard to see obstacles. Start with a quick room-by-room safety check; it’s amazing what you can spot when you’re looking with a critical eye. Once you’ve identified those trip hazards, either fix them or remove them entirely. Improving your lighting, especially in hallways, stairwells, and entryways, can make a world of difference. And don't forget those often-overlooked areas like bathrooms and stairs — adding grab bars and handrails there can provide much-needed support. After you’ve made some changes, take another walk through your home and reassess. If you're still having issues, or if you're dealing with repeated falls, it might be time to bring in a professional for a more thorough assessment. They can help identify structural changes or more involved modifications you might need.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
